      "message": "Count what an owner is holding, not what they last asked for\n\nFour ways to walk past a limit, each of them the same mistake: the\ncount was taken of a state rather than of a commitment, so changing the\nstate let go of the count without letting go of the thing.\n\nAgents were counted while active, and coming back was not checked. So\nthe limit was one on registering rather than on having: retire what you\nhave, register that many more, bring the first ones back. Reactivating\nnow has to fit, the way registering does.\n\nOpen tasks were counted while open, and claiming one moved it out of\nthe count. A task somebody is working on is more of a commitment than\none nobody has picked up, so claimed work counts too — and putting a\nfinished task back into play asks for the room again, since that is\ntaking the work back up.\n\nOwnership moving asked nothing of the owner it moved to. Offer\neverything you hold to an account with room, accept it there, and both\nthe repository limit and the disk limit were beside the point.\nAccepting now asks whether it fits, in repositories and on disk.\n\nAnd deleting a repository left its open tasks belonging to nobody,\ncounted against nobody, forever — so a repository made, filled with\ntasks and deleted was any number of open tasks. Work in a repository\nthat is gone is over now. The text stays, because knowledge does.\n\nChange-Id: Icounting0103\nSigned-off-by: Mandip Adhikari <92096340+mandipadk@users.noreply.github.com>",
